// AN OPERATOR THE CORE DOES NOT DEFINE IS A METHOD CALL. `Lower.coreBinOp` names the operators that
// are instructions; everything else lowers to `Invoke`, exactly as an ALPHANUMERIC infix operator
// already did (`a to b` is `a.to(b)`). The kernel gains nothing, and an operator becomes a library
// matter — the same answer the owner took for interpolators.
//
// `++` IS THE REGRESSION GUARD. It used to have an arm of its own that built precisely this Invoke;
// that arm is deleted, so if the general rule is ever narrowed these two lines fail first.

def main(): Unit =
  println(List(1, 2) ++ List(3))
  // `"a" ++ "b"` is NOT here: the executor has no `++` on a String, before this change and after —
  // measured byte-identical on origin/main — so putting it in would test v2's bridge, not this rule.

  // the core operators must still be INSTRUCTIONS, not calls
  println(1 + 2)
  println(7 - 2)
  println(3 * 4)
  println(9 / 2)
  println(5 % 3)
  println(1 << 4)
  println(16 >> 2)
  println(6 & 3)
  println(6 | 3)
  println(6 ^ 3)
  println(2 < 3)
  println(2 == 2)
  println(2 != 3)

  // unary `!` is a different node and must not be routed anywhere
  val flag = false
  println(!flag)
